## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design a CKNE-aware telemetry pipeline that scales across many tenants while respecting SLA and fairness. It probes understanding of mapping CKNE to resource commitments, prioritization strategies, and dynamic reallocation under load.

## Key Concepts

- **CKNE-to-detector-depth mapping**: Higher CKNE correlates with deeper inspection capabilities, impacting latency and compute cost
- **Priority queuing**: Critical tenants receive preferential treatment during resource contention
- **Per-tenant ingestion caps**: Rate limiting ensures fair resource distribution
- **Degradation loop**: Automated capacity reclamation maintains system stability
- **SLA guarantees**: Baseline service levels must be preserved regardless of tenant priority

## Design Considerations

The solution balances two competing priorities: maximizing security coverage for critical tenants while maintaining baseline observability for all. The degradation loop prioritizes high-CKNE tenants but protects low-CKNE tenants from complete starvation through minimum SLA guarantees.

## Code Example

```javascript
function detectorDepth(ckne) {
  return 1 + Math.floor(ckne * 4);
}

function ingestionCap(ckne) {
  return 1000 + (ckne * 5000);
}

async function degradationLoop(tenants, availableDetectors) {
  const targetDetectors = tenants.reduce((sum, t) => sum + detectorDepth(t.ckne), 0);
  const deficit = targetDetectors - availableDetectors;

  if (deficit <= 0) return;

  const lowPriority = tenants
    .filter(t => t.utilization < 0.3 && t.ckne < 0.5)
    .sort((a, b) => a.ckne - b.ckne);

  for (const tenant of lowPriority) {
    if (deficit <= 0) break;
    tenant.throttleRate = Math.min(tenant.throttleRate + 0.1, 0.5);
    deficit -= 1;
  }
}
```

